E=[('Opening · Romans 1:20 whole verse', 4, 'has been clearly perceived in the things that have been made."', 'has been clearly perceived in the things that have been made. So they are without excuse;"'), ("Opening · pick up Ch. 11's Bridge instead of repeating it", 19, "Chapter 10 ended with a call to become saints—to let God's perfect love transform us so completely that others encounter Christ through our witness.", 'That chapter ended where this one begins: a Law of Love this real requires a Lawgiver.'), ("Opening · pick up Ch. 11's Bridge instead of repeating it", 23, "We've been building on the claim that love is a law as real and universal as gravity. But laws require lawgivers. If that claim is wrong, the entire architecture collapses. So the question is not peripheral: it is the question on which everything else depends.", 'If there is no Lawgiver, the entire architecture collapses. This is the question on which everything else depends.'), ("Opening · pick up Ch. 11's Bridge instead of repeating it", 25, "If love is truly a law—as universal and binding as the law of gravity—how do we know it's not just evolutionary programming or cultural convention?", 'And how do we know the law of love is not just evolutionary programming or cultural convention?'), ("Opening · reason's witness, not a proof that replaces faith (CCC 31, 35)", 27, 'we can reason our way to God from the reality of love itself.', 'we can reason our way to God from the reality of love itself. The Church calls such reasoning "converging and convincing arguments" (CCC 31); they do not replace faith, but they "can predispose one to faith and help one to see that faith is not opposed to reason" (CCC 35).'), ('The moral argument · not "classic": the three-step form is a modern one', 29, '## The Moral Argument: Classic Form', '## The Moral Argument'), ('The moral argument · not "classic": the three-step form is a modern one', 31, 'The classic moral argument goes like this:', 'Put simply, the moral argument goes like this:'), ("Premise 1 · facets, chapter subject, Ratzinger's title and source", 47, "in Chapter 5 and traced through each pillar in Chapters 6–9—Cardinal Ratzinger's warning, on the eve of his election as Benedict XVI,", "in Chapter 5, on how love binds, and traced through truth, justice, mercy and sacrifice in Chapters 6–9—Cardinal Joseph Ratzinger's warning, on the eve of his election as Pope Benedict XVI,"), ("Premise 1 · facets, chapter subject, Ratzinger's title and source", 47, 'one\'s own ego and desires" (quoted in Chapter 5).', 'one\'s own ego and desires" (Homily, Mass *Pro Eligendo Romano Pontifice*, April 18, 2005).'), ('Premise 1 · Evangelium Vitae: two sections had been spliced into one quotation', 47, 'St. John Paul II traced the same line, and named its cost, a decade earlier:', 'St. John Paul II traced the same line a decade earlier: "when the sense of God is lost, the sense of man is also threatened and poisoned" (*Evangelium Vitae* 22). And he named its cost:'), ('Premise 1 · Evangelium Vitae: two sections had been spliced into one quotation', 50, '"When the sense of God is lost, the sense of man is also threatened… any reference to common values and to a truth absolutely binding on everyone is lost, and social life ventures onto the shifting sands of complete relativism. Everything is negotiable,', '"In this way, any reference to common values and to a truth absolutely binding on everyone is lost, and social life ventures on to the shifting sands of complete relativism. At that point, everything is negotiable,'), ('Premise 2 · banned payment word', 77, 'mercy extended, sacrifice paid.', 'mercy extended, sacrifice offered.'), ('Euthyphro · accurate source; chapter subject; the Catholic answer through natural law', 85, "going back to Plato's *Euthyphro* dialogue:", "adapted from a question Socrates asks in Plato's *Euthyphro* dialogue:"), ('Euthyphro · accurate source; chapter subject; the Catholic answer through natural law', 85, 'Chapter 3 already answered it:', "Chapter 3, on love's sacred source, already answered it:"), ('Euthyphro · accurate source; chapter subject; the Catholic answer through natural law', 85, 'they reveal the Good that He eternally is.', 'they reveal the Good that He eternally is. And the moral law in our hearts is a share in that same Good: St. Thomas Aquinas calls the natural law "the rational creature\'s participation of the eternal law" (*Summa Theologiae* I-II, q.91, a.2), that is, of God\'s own wisdom ordering all things, imprinted on the minds He made.'), ("Why only God · the Ch. 1 quotation did not exist; Ch. 1's actual words", 95, 'Chapter 1 already named the pattern — even sin is "choosing a perceived good for ourselves at the expense of others," never a choice of no good at all.', 'Chapter 1, on the problem of evil, already named the pattern: sin is "choosing a lesser good over the greatest Good," never a choice of no good at all.'), ('Why only God · Aquinas quoted as he wrote it', 99, 'our love "is not the cause of the goodness of things... whereas the love of God infuses and creates goodness"', 'our love for anything "is not the cause of its goodness," whereas "the love of God infuses and creates goodness"'), ('Atheist\'s Dilemma · "debt" framing', 107, 'for honesty is a debt truth collects everywhere:', 'for truth asks honesty of us everywhere:'), ('Natural law · Romans 2:15 whole verse', 115, 'while their conscience also bears witness" (Romans 2:14-15).', 'while their conscience also bears witness and their conflicting thoughts accuse or perhaps excuse them" (Romans 2:14-15).'), ('Natural law · overclaim: an atheist can ground it in more than opinion, just not in anything that binds', 119, 'but they cannot ground that wrong in anything beyond their own opinions—and an opinion, however strong, cannot make a wrong objective.', 'but, as we have just seen, they cannot ground that wrong in anything with the authority to bind us.'), ("Natural law · the Buddhist/atheist question in the Church's own words (CCC 846–848, 1257; LG 16)", 121, "The Church's answer is careful and honest. The four pillars of love are not", 'The Church\'s answer is careful and honest. All salvation comes from Christ: "the one Christ is the mediator and the way of salvation; he is present to us in his body which is the Church" (CCC 846). Truth, justice, mercy and sacrifice are not'), ("Natural law · the Buddhist/atheist question in the Church's own words (CCC 846–848, 1257; LG 16)", 121, "This follows the same logic as the sacraments: God binds *us* to the sacraments as the ordinary means of grace — but God is not Himself bound by them. He can pour grace through any channel He chooses. Those who follow the light they have been given — the natural law, the grace active in conscience, the partial truth written on their hearts — are, in a real sense, doing the will of the Father, even without yet knowing the Father's name. God's perfect justice gives each person what they are due, accounting for what was offered to them and not what was withheld through no fault of their own (CCC 1257, 1260; *Lumen Gentium* 16). But none of this cancels the necessity of the Church", '"God has bound salvation to the sacrament of Baptism, but he himself is not bound by his sacraments" (CCC 1257). And so the Church teaches that "those who, through no fault of their own, do not know the Gospel of Christ or his Church, but who nevertheless seek God with a sincere heart, and, moved by grace, try in their actions to do his will as they know it through the dictates of their conscience — those too may achieve eternal salvation" (CCC 847, quoting *Lumen Gentium* 16). Such people are, in a real sense, doing the will of the Father, even without yet knowing the Father\'s name, and whatever grace reaches them is Christ\'s grace. The Council is just as honest that "often men, deceived by the Evil One, have become vain in their reasonings" (*Lumen Gentium* 16), and the Church "still has the obligation and also the sacred right to evangelize all men" (CCC 848). None of this cancels the necessity of the Church'), ('Integration · every facet; one in God (todo 61); no "intelligent design" label', 125, "The way love integrates truth, justice, mercy, sacrifice, covenant, life-giving, and communion into one coherent whole points to intelligent design. These facets could conflict—truth could oppose mercy, justice could contradict love—but in God's perfect love, they integrate perfectly.", "The way love holds together what is sacred, covenant, truth, justice, mercy, sacrifice and life-giving communion as one coherent whole points to intelligence. These facets could conflict—truth could oppose mercy, justice could crush it—but in God's perfect love they are not parts kept in balance; they are one."), ('Personal encounter · Romans 1:20 by reference, not partial quotation', 129, 'As St. Paul writes, God\'s "invisible nature, namely, his eternal power and deity, has been clearly perceived in the things that have been made" (Romans 1:20).', "As the verse that opens this chapter says, God's eternal power and deity have been clearly perceived in the things He has made (cf. Romans 1:20)."), ('Personal encounter · not every unbeliever is culpable; Romans 1:18 with cf.', 131, 'Yet some still don\'t believe—not because the evidence is insufficient, but because "they suppress the truth" (Romans 1:18).', "Yet some still don't believe—not because the evidence is insufficient. Sometimes the reason is a wound or an ignorance that is no fault of their own; sometimes it is what St. Paul warns of, holding down a truth we would rather not face (cf. Romans 1:18)."), ('Personal encounter · sacrifice as self-gift, said of God rightly (todo 61)', 135, "Every time you sacrifice for another, you're participating in God's nature.", "Every time you give yourself for another, you're sharing in the self-giving love that God is."), ('Personal encounter · John 8:31 was partial', 137, 'As Jesus promised, "If you continue in my word, you are truly my disciples, and you will know the truth, and the truth will make you free" (John 8:31-32).', 'As Jesus promised, those who continue in His word will know the truth, and the truth will make them free (cf. John 8:31-32).'), ('Law of the Gospel · the natural law and the New Law are not the same law (CCC 1965–1966)', 145, "The Catholic Church gives us a beautiful name for this law: **the Law of the Gospel** (CCC 1965-1974). This is not a burden imposed from outside, but God's own interior law of love written on our hearts through the Holy Spirit.", 'And in Christ, God brings that law to its fullness. The Church calls it **the New Law**, or **the Law of the Gospel**: "the perfection here on earth of the divine law, natural and revealed" (CCC 1965). It is not a burden imposed from outside, but the interior law of love that the Holy Spirit writes on the hearts of those who believe (CCC 1965-1966).'), ('Law of the Gospel · 1 John 4:18 was partial; CCC 1972 is the law of love "rather than from fear"', 153, 'It is not followed out of fear, but out of love, for "there is no fear in love, but perfect love casts out fear" (1 John 4:18).', 'It is not followed out of fear, but out of love (CCC 1972), for perfect love casts out fear (cf. 1 John 4:18).'), ('Law of the Gospel · the quotation is CCC 1983, not 1972', 159, 'operating through charity" (CCC 1972).', 'operating through charity" (CCC 1983).'), ('Beatitudes · banned word; source given', 173, '(*Jesus of Nazareth*, 2007)', '(*Jesus of Nazareth*, 2007, ch. 4)'), ('Beatitudes · banned word; source given', 173, 'To live the Beatitudes is not to perform eight tasks', 'To live the Beatitudes is not to complete eight tasks'), ('Beatitudes · "the four facets"', 175, 'the four facets of perfect love we have been tracing are all here', 'truth, justice, mercy and sacrifice, which we have been tracing all along, are all here'), ('Beatitudes · each Beatitude quoted whole', 177, '*"Blessed are the poor in spirit"* and *"the pure in heart, for they shall see God."*', '*"Blessed are the poor in spirit, for theirs is the kingdom of heaven"* and *"Blessed are the pure in heart, for they shall see God."*'), ('Beatitudes · each Beatitude quoted whole', 179, '*"Blessed are those who hunger and thirst for righteousness."*', '*"Blessed are those who hunger and thirst for righteousness, for they shall be satisfied."*'), ('Beatitudes · each Beatitude quoted whole; no "pays"', 183, '*"Blessed are the meek"* and *"those who are persecuted for righteousness\' sake."*', '*"Blessed are the meek, for they shall inherit the earth"* and *"Blessed are those who are persecuted for righteousness\' sake, for theirs is the kingdom of heaven."*'), ('Beatitudes · each Beatitude quoted whole; no "pays"', 183, 'love that costs everything and pays it anyway.', 'love that costs everything and gives it anyway.'), ('Beatitudes · each Beatitude quoted whole; shalom defined', 185, 'The peacemaker—*"they shall be called sons of God"*—is', 'The peacemaker—*"Blessed are the peacemakers, for they shall be called sons of God"*—is'), ('Beatitudes · each Beatitude quoted whole; shalom defined', 185, 'This is *shalom*: the communion', "This is *shalom*, the Bible's word for peace as wholeness: the communion"), ('Beatitudes · Matthew 5:12 whole', 187, 'for your reward is great in heaven"* (Matthew 5:11-12).', 'for your reward is great in heaven, for so men persecuted the prophets who were before you"* (Matthew 5:11-12).'), ('Beatitudes · Matthew 5:48 by reference; chapter subject; banned word', 191, 'This is the "be perfect, as your heavenly Father is perfect" of Matthew 5:48 (Chapter 10), now given flesh: not flawless performance, but a heart so conformed to Christ that His own blessedness - His own love - radiates through us.', 'This is the call to be perfect as our heavenly Father is perfect (cf. Matthew 5:48; Chapter 10, on communion), now given flesh: not a flawless record, but a heart so conformed to Christ that His own blessedness—His own love—radiates through us.'), ('Your mission · which law is already written on every heart', 197, "Every person you meet already lives according to this law imperfectly—it's the water they swim in.", "Every person you meet already lives, imperfectly, by the natural law written on their heart—it's the water they swim in."), ('Your mission · paraphrase marked with cf.', 207, 'to fulfill it (Matthew 5:17) came also that we might have life—and so love—in abundance (John 10:10).', 'to fulfill it (cf. Matthew 5:17) came also that we might have life—and so love—in abundance (cf. John 10:10).'), ('The Bridge · matches the Ch. 13 H1, says what it is about, no "pillars"', 211, '## The Bridge to Virtues', '## The Bridge to Faith Working Through Love'), ('The Bridge · matches the Ch. 13 H1, says what it is about, no "pillars"', 213, 'In Chapter 13, we turn to the virtues—faith working through love—and see how each one upholds all four pillars.', 'In Chapter 13, "Faith Works Through Love," we turn to the virtues, the steady habits through which faith works through love (cf. Galatians 5:6), and see how each one holds truth, justice, mercy and sacrifice together.'), ('Further Study · partial quotes become descriptions; entries for every source cited', 222, '- **John 13:34:** "A new commandment I give to you, that you love one another; even as I have loved you" - Jesus establishes the New Law', "- **John 13:34:** Jesus gives the new commandment, to love one another as He has loved us - He establishes the New Law\n- **John 8:31-32:** Those who continue in Christ's word will know the truth, and the truth will make them free\n- **Matthew 5:17, 48; 7:2:** Christ comes to fulfil the law, not to abolish it; the call to be perfect as the heavenly Father is perfect; the measure we give is the measure we get"), ('Further Study · partial quotes become descriptions; entries for every source cited', 224, '- **Jeremiah 31:33:** "I will put my law within them, and I will write it upon their hearts" - prophecy of the Law of the Gospel', '- **Jeremiah 31:33:** God promises a new covenant in which He will put His law within His people and write it upon their hearts - the prophecy the Law of the Gospel fulfills (CCC 1965)'), ('Further Study · partial quotes become descriptions; entries for every source cited', 228, '- **Psalm 19:1-4:** "The heavens are telling the glory of God; and the firmament proclaims his handiwork" - creation\'s silent witness to the Creator', '- **Psalm 19:1-4:** Creation\'s silent witness to the Creator, opening with "The heavens are telling the glory of God; and the firmament proclaims his handiwork" (v. 1)'), ('Further Study · partial quotes become descriptions; entries for every source cited', 229, '- **1 John 4:7-8, 16:** "Beloved, let us love one another; for love is of God... God is love, and he who abides in love abides in God, and God abides in him"', '- **1 John 4:7-8, 16:** Love comes from God, for God is love, and whoever abides in love abides in God\n- **1 John 4:18:** Perfect love casts out fear - the New Law is a law of love, not of fear (CCC 1972)\n- **John 10:10:** Christ came that we may have life, and have it abundantly'), ('Further Study · CCC entries corrected and added', 235, '- **CCC 1972:** The New Law is the grace of the Holy Spirit given through faith in Christ, operating through charity', "- **CCC 1966, 1972, 1983:** The New Law is the grace of the Holy Spirit received by faith in Christ, operating through charity (1983); a law of love, of grace and of freedom (1972)\n- **CCC 846-848:** All salvation comes from Christ through His Church; those who, through no fault of their own, do not know Him; the Church's duty to evangelize all\n- **CCC 1257-1260:** The necessity of Baptism, and God who is not bound by His sacraments"), ('Further Study · titles; every thinker the chapter cites', 241, "- **St. Thomas Aquinas (1225-1274):** His *Summa Theologiae* presents the classical arguments for God's existence and explains natural law as participation in God's eternal law; his treatment of the virtues shows how they align us with God's nature", "- **St. Thomas Aquinas, Doctor of the Church (1225-1274):** His *Summa Theologiae* presents the classical arguments for God's existence (I, q.2, a.3), explains natural law as participation in God's eternal law (I-II, q.91, a.2), and teaches that God's love creates the goodness it loves (I, q.20, a.2); his treatment of the virtues shows how they align us with God's nature\n- **Pope Benedict XVI (1927-2022):** *Jesus of Nazareth* (2007), ch. 4, reads the Sermon on the Mount and the Beatitudes as a portrait of Christ Himself"), ('Further Study · titles; every thinker the chapter cites', 242, '- **Peter Kreeft (1937-present):** His *A Refutation of Moral Relativism*', '- **Peter Kreeft (1937-present), Catholic philosopher at Boston College:** His *A Refutation of Moral Relativism*'), ('Further Study · Church documents the chapter quotes', 242, 'making it accessible to modern readers', 'making it accessible to modern readers\n\n### Encyclicals & Church Documents\n- **Second Vatican Council, *Lumen Gentium* 16 (1964):** How those who have not yet received the Gospel are related to the People of God\n- **St. John Paul II, *Evangelium Vitae* 20-22 (1995):** How losing the sense of God threatens the sense of man and leaves even the right to life open to bargaining\n- **Dicastery for the Doctrine of the Faith, *Dignitas Infinita* 15 (2024):** Human dignity is not conferred by others; it is prior to any recognition and cannot be lost\n- **Cardinal Joseph Ratzinger, Homily at the Mass *Pro Eligendo Romano Pontifice* (April 18, 2005):** The "dictatorship of relativism"')]
